<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="runbooks"
      toc={[
        {"shortest-path", "The shortest path"},
        {"create", "1. Create or import a draft"},
        {"author", "2. Define the workflow"},
        {"publish", "3. Publish a resolvable version"},
        {"start", "4. Review and start the run"},
        {"approve", "5. Approve the execution"},
        {"results", "6. Read the result"},
        {"definition", "Definition v1 reference"},
        {"failure", "Failure and safety"},
        {"agents", "LLMs and MCP"},
        {"limits", "Limits"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="runbooks">
        <:dek>
          Build a procedure from declared actions, publish it only when every target resolves, then
          run one frozen plan through policy and approval. Stages stop at the first failure; each
          command, output, and decision remains reviewable.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          At least one
          <.link
            href="/docs/deployment"
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >runner</.link>
          enrolled in the account and assigned to the groups the runbook will target.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          The required
          <.link
            href="/docs/action-packs"
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >action packs</.link>
          installed and trusted on those runners.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          Permission to manage or run runbooks. If policy requires approval, an approver whose
          runner scope covers the complete plan.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_h2 id="shortest-path">The shortest path</.docs_h2>
      <ol class="mt-5 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base text-zinc-400 marker:text-zinc-600">
        <li>
          Create a draft in <.link
            href="/app/runbooks"
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >
            Runbooks
          </.link>, or import a canonical JSON definition.
        </li>
        <li>
          Add operator context and run-time inputs, then define each step's action, arguments,
          outputs, success conditions, and optional wait.
        </li>
        <li>
          Select the target scope, then choose an action available across that complete scope.
        </li>
        <li>Publish after the current definition and live preflight both pass.</li>
        <li>Supply the run-time values and reason, review the resolved plan, and start it.</li>
        <li>
          Approve the complete execution once when policy requires it, then review each result.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h2 id="create">1. Create or import a draft</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        Open
        <.link href="/app/runbooks" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">Runbooks</.link>
        and choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">New runbook</strong>. Start with a short title,
        a stable slug, and the operator context: prerequisites, stop conditions, ownership, the
        expected outcome, and rollback instructions. Context supports headings, paragraphs, lists,
        and fenced code. Links, images, and raw HTML stay inert.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        If another tool or an LLM already produced Definition v1 JSON, choose <.link
          href="/app/runbooks/import"
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >
          Import runbook
        </.link>.
        Drop or select a <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">.json</code>
        file, or paste the document. An import always becomes an editable draft; it never publishes
        or runs automatically.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/runbooks/import.webp"
        preview_h="max-h-[32rem]"
        title="emisar console · Import runbook"
        caption="Import a file or paste the same canonical JSON used by the console and MCP."
        alt="The emisar Import runbook page with a title field, JSON file drop area, canonical JSON text area, and Import as draft button."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="author">2. Define the workflow</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_h3 id="run-time-inputs">Run-time inputs</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        Declare only the values that change between executions. An input can be a string, integer,
        number, boolean, or enum; make it required, constrain its range or length, or give it a
        same-type default. An integer is a whole number, while a number may contain a decimal.
        Sensitive inputs have no stored default and appear as
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">[REDACTED]</code>
        in plans, approvals, and results.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/runbooks/inputs.webp"
        title="emisar console · Run-time inputs"
        caption="The example accepts one validated configuration path and supplies a safe default."
        alt="The Edge configuration rollout editor showing the config_path string input, its description, required and sensitive checkboxes, default value, and length bounds."
      />

      <.docs_h3 id="stages-and-steps">Stages and steps</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base text-zinc-400">
        A runbook executes stages from top to bottom. A stage groups work behind one barrier. A
        step declares one action, its runner or group targets, argument bindings, retained outputs,
        success conditions, and an optional wait policy.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Sequential</strong>
            finishes one step across all selected runners before the next step starts.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Parallel</strong>
            makes all stage items eligible together. Maximum concurrency limits the number of
            active attempts; use <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">1</code>
            for one-host-at-a-time maintenance.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Every stage is a barrier.</strong>
            The next stage starts only after every item in the current stage succeeds.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        Select targets before choosing the action. The action menu contains only work currently
        available across the complete selection. The editor follows the action to its pack; you do
        not pin a pack version in the definition.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/runbooks/stage.webp"
        title="emisar console · Stage editor"
        caption="The reload stage fans one declared action across the edge group, with at most two attempts active."
        alt="The first stage of the Edge configuration rollout runbook showing its identifier, title, parallel execution, concurrency of two, runner group target, Caddy reload action, and file argument binding."
      />

      <.docs_h3 id="target-selection">Target selection</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base text-zinc-400">
        A step can run on one named runner, every online runner in a group, or one online runner
        from a group. Use a runner name for host-specific work, the complete group for fleet
        changes and checks, and one group member for representative diagnostics or a canary.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span><strong class="text-zinc-200">Runner name</strong> freezes that runner.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Every runner in group</strong>
            expands the group to one logical item per online member.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">One random runner</strong>
            accepts one group and freezes one member during preflight.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        One-runner selection still validates the action, trusted pack, and compatible contract on
        every online member of the group before choosing. The start page and the execution use the
        same frozen runner. If that runner disconnects before dispatch, the execution stops; it
        does not move the work to another member.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/runbooks/targets.webp"
        title="emisar console · Target selection"
        caption="Choose every online runner in a group, one online member, or a named runner from the same picker."
        alt="The target picker showing the app-api group with Every runner in group, One random runner, and api-iad-02 as peer choices."
      />
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Target forms">{~s({"selection":"all","refs":["runner:nomad-hvn02~0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef"]}
{"selection":"all","refs":["group:va1-nomad"]}
{"selection":"random_one","refs":["group:va1-nomad"]})}</.docs_code>

      <.docs_h3 id="action-arguments">Action arguments</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base text-zinc-400">
        Each action argument receives one complete JSON value. Omit an optional argument, enter a
        literal, bind a declared run-time input, or use a named output from an earlier stage. There
        is no string interpolation. The editor checks the bound value against the trusted action
        schema before publication and again when it resolves a run.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/runbooks/arguments.webp"
        title="emisar console · Action arguments"
        caption="The reload step binds the action's file argument to the config_path run-time input."
        alt="The Arguments section for the reload_caddy step showing the optional file JSON argument bound to the config_path run-time input."
      />

      <.docs_h3 id="extracted-outputs">Extracted outputs</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base text-zinc-400">
        Retain only the values that later work or a success condition needs. Read structured output,
        stdout, or stderr, then extract with JSON Pointer, contains, literal grep, or a bounded
        regular expression. Give each value a stable output ID. Mark an output sensitive to mask it
        in results and evidence while still allowing conditions and compatible sensitive arguments
        in later stages to use the real value.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/runbooks/outputs.webp"
        title="emisar console · Extracted outputs"
        caption="The health check retains only the healthy field from structured output."
        alt="The Extracted outputs section for the check_upstreams step showing the healthy output read from structured output with JSON Pointer expression slash healthy."
      />

      <.docs_h3 id="success-conditions">Success conditions</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base text-zinc-400">
        An action that exits successfully counts as a successful step unless you add conditions.
        Conditions read named extracted outputs; every condition must pass. Compare with equality,
        ordering, containment, membership, or a bounded regular expression. Add the output first so
        the condition cannot refer to an undefined value.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/runbooks/conditions.webp"
        title="emisar console · Success conditions"
        caption="The upstream check succeeds only when its extracted healthy value is true."
        alt="The Success conditions section for the check_upstreams step showing healthy must equal the JSON value true."
      />

      <.docs_h3 id="wait-policy">Wait policy</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base text-zinc-400">
        When a successful observation does not yet meet its conditions, a wait can run that same
        observation again after a fixed interval. Bound it with both a timeout and a maximum number
        of observations. A wait never retries an action failure, denial, refusal, extraction error,
        timeout, or cancellation; those halt the execution.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/runbooks/wait.webp"
        title="emisar console · Wait policy"
        caption="The health check observes again every 10 seconds, for at most 12 observations or 120 seconds."
        alt="The expanded Retry policy section for the check_upstreams step showing Observe again, a 10 second interval, a 120 second timeout, and 12 maximum observations."
      />

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Identifiers are references, not labels.">
        Input IDs name run-time values. Step and output IDs form prior-output references such as <code>check_health.status</code>. Stage and step IDs also remain stable in the frozen plan,
        result, and audit trail. Titles are the human-readable text you can revise.
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="publish">3. Publish a resolvable version</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        Save whenever you need to keep incomplete work. Drafts are not runnable. Publishing has a
        higher bar: the strict definition must be valid, every target must resolve in your scope,
        each selected runner must expose one compatible action contract, and the current trusted
        pack and policy plan must resolve. The editor lists every issue beside
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Publish check</strong>
        and disables Publish until the check
        passes.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        Publishing freezes that definition as <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">slug@version</code>.
        Editing a published runbook creates the next draft; existing executions keep the exact
        version and plan they started with. If runners, actions, or packs later drift, a new
        execution is blocked until you publish a working version.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="start">4. Review and start the run</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        Choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">Run</strong>
        on a published runbook. Supply its
        run-time inputs and a reason that explains why the procedure should run now. Before
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Start runbook</strong>
        becomes available, emisar resolves the
        exact runner for every item, the current trusted pack and content hash, action arguments,
        policy decisions, and stage concurrency.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        Read the plan as the dispatch receipt. A one-runner group item names both the selected
        runner and its source group. The run will not silently add or replace a target, reinterpret
        an argument, or move work between stages after it starts.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/runbooks/start.webp"
        title="emisar console · Start execution"
        caption="The start page summarizes the resolved work and whether one run-level approval will be required."
        alt="The Edge configuration rollout start page showing operator context, run-time values, the first plan stage, three actions, two stages, required run approval, and recent executions."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="approve">5. Approve the execution</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        Runbooks use the same
        <.link
          href="/docs/policies-and-approvals"
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >account policy</.link>
        as direct actions. If any item requires approval, emisar opens <strong class="text-zinc-200">one approval for the complete frozen execution</strong>.
        The approver reviews every stage, action, runner, visible argument, and risk in one place.
        Approval does not create a second approval for each action.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        No action run is created before approval. The decision applies only to this execution, and
        the approver's runner scope must cover the complete plan. Current access, policy, pack
        trust, action compatibility, and runner state are checked again before dispatch.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/runbooks/approval.webp"
        preview_h="max-h-[32rem]"
        title="emisar console · Runbook approval"
        caption="One decision covers the complete frozen plan; the action rows keep targets and visible arguments close to the work being approved."
        alt="A pending Edge configuration rollout approval showing the frozen two-stage plan, Caddy actions, target runners, visible file argument, and run-level approve or deny decision."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="results">6. Read the result</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        The execution page follows the same stage order as the plan. Each item shows the action,
        risk, runner, step ID, outcome, duration, and its bounded command and output. Expand
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">View</strong>
        for the complete action run when you need event-level detail. Sensitive values remain
        redacted.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        When a step extracts outputs or defines success conditions, the result also keeps the
        extracted values, integrity digest, condition evidence, observation attempts, and wait
        state. This is the evidence to use when deciding whether to continue outside the runbook or
        start a rollback.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/runbooks/result.webp"
        preview_h="max-h-[32rem]"
        title="emisar console · Runbook result"
        caption="Executed items keep commands and bounded output inline; the upstream check also records its extracted value and condition evidence."
        alt="A successful Edge configuration rollout result showing Caddy command output followed by the healthy extracted output and passed success condition evidence."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="definition">Definition v1 reference</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        JSON is the only interchange format. The structured editor, import flow, persistence layer,
        and MCP use the same strict
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">schema_version: 1</code>
        object; unknown fields are rejected. This is a smaller version of the edge rollout used
        throughout this guide:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Canonical runbook JSON">{~s({
  "schema_version": 1,
  "context_markdown": "## Before you run\\n\\nValidate the candidate Caddyfile.",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "id": "config_path",
      "description": "Absolute path to the validated Caddy configuration.",
      "type": "string",
      "required": false,
      "sensitive": false,
      "default": "/etc/caddy/Caddyfile",
      "min_length": 1,
      "max_length": 256
    }
  ],
  "stages": [
    {
      "id": "reload",
      "title": "Reload edge configuration",
      "mode": "parallel",
      "max_parallel": 2,
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "reload_caddy",
          "pack": {"id": "caddy"},
          "action": "caddy.reload_config",
          "targets": {"selection": "all", "refs": ["group:edge-web"]},
          "args": {
            "file": {"source": "input", "ref": "config_path"}
          },
          "outputs": [],
          "success": [],
          "wait": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "verify",
      "title": "Verify the edge fleet",
      "mode": "parallel",
      "max_parallel": 2,
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "check_upstreams",
          "pack": {"id": "caddy"},
          "action": "caddy.reverse_proxy_upstreams",
          "targets": {"selection": "all", "refs": ["group:edge-web"]},
          "args": {},
          "outputs": [
            {
              "id": "healthy",
              "source": "structured_output",
              "sensitive": false,
              "extract": {
                "type": "json_pointer",
                "expression": "/healthy"
              }
            }
          ],
          "success": [
            {
              "output": "healthy",
              "operator": "equals",
              "value": true
            }
          ],
          "wait": {
            "interval_seconds": 10,
            "timeout_seconds": 120,
            "max_attempts": 12
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
})}</.docs_code>

      <.docs_h2 id="failure">Failure and safety</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        A denial, refusal, timeout, cancellation, action failure, extraction failure, unmet terminal
        condition, lost authorization, or unusable frozen pack halts the execution. Peers already
        running may settle and report their real outcomes, but no new item and no later stage
        starts.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        Approval never overrides later drift. Before every physical attempt, emisar rechecks runner
        access, current membership, policy, pack trust, the action contract, and runner state.
        Cancelling an execution also cancels pending stages, waits, and active attempts without
        erasing their history.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:warn} title="Signature-enforcing runners are not supported yet.">
        Customer-CA signing currently covers exact direct <code>run_action</code>
        dispatches. A runbook that targets a signature-enforcing runner fails closed with <code>signed_runbook_unsupported</code>; use individually signed actions for that runner.
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="agents">LLMs and MCP</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        Connected agents see the same definitions and subject-scoped results through <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">list_runbooks</code>, <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">get_runbook</code>, <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">execute_runbook</code>, and <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">create_runbook_draft</code>.
        Draft creation never publishes or executes. While work or approval remains, the response
        names the blocking reason and returns an exact
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">wait_for_run</code>
        continuation.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        For client setup and the complete tool contract, continue to <.link
          href="/docs/connect-an-llm"
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Connect an LLM</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="limits">Limits</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        One definition is at most 64 KiB and contains up to 32 inputs, 16 stages, 32 steps in total,
        and 16 target refs per step. A resolved execution contains at most 256 logical items. A
        parallel stage can run 1 through 16 attempts at once — 5 unless the definition sets it. Each
        step can retain 16 outputs and evaluate 16 success conditions. A wait polls every 5 seconds
        to 1 hour and makes 2 to 100 attempts. The frozen execution plan is at most 1 MiB. An
        execution, including waits, ends within 24 hours.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        See the
        <.link
          href="/docs/limits"
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >limits reference</.link>
        for the wider API, output, and retention caps.
      </p>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Keep investigation outside the runbook.">
        Use a runbook when the stages, stopping rules, and expected result are known. Keep
        open-ended diagnosis in the action catalog, where an operator or agent can inspect evidence
        and choose the next exact action.
      </.docs_callout>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
